// =============================================================================
// Row-Level Security (RLS) Roles -- Power BI Desktop "Manage Roles"
// =============================================================================
// Deliberately mirrors the Snowflake role model 1:1 (snowflake/07_rbac_roles.sql,
// snowflake/08_row_access_policies.sql) rather than inventing a separate
// Power BI-only access model -- the same person's Snowflake role and Power BI
// role should always answer "what can this person see" identically,
// regardless of which tool they're looking at.
//
// IMPORTANT: for Import-mode tables (every page except Customer Drilldown,
// see docs/13-power-bi.md Section 5), RLS filters the IMPORTED data cache,
// re-evaluated per session against the viewer's identity (via
// USERPRINCIPALNAME() mapped through the same collector_team_map table
// Snowflake's row access policy uses -- one mapping table, two engines
// reading it, not two definitions of "who's on which team" to keep in sync).

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Role: Executive
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Table filter applied: fct_delinquency, fct_payment, fct_contact,
// fct_promise_to_pay, dim_customer -- ALL set to FALSE (no rows visible).
// Mirrors Snowflake's ROLE_EXECUTIVE having no GRANT on MARTS at all
// (Phase 12 Section 5) -- executives only ever see the pre-aggregated KPI
// table/measures above, never a row-level fact or a customer record.
//
//   dim_customer:      FALSE
//   fct_delinquency:   FALSE
//   fct_payment:       FALSE
//   fct_contact:       FALSE
//   fct_promise_to_pay: FALSE
//
// (KPI summary tables/measures remain fully visible -- RLS is applied to
// the underlying fact/dim tables only, matching exactly which Snowflake
// schema ROLE_EXECUTIVE was granted vs. denied.)

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Role: Collections Manager
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// dim_collector filter:
[team_name] =
    LOOKUPVALUE (
        collector_team_map[team_name],
        collector_team_map[snowflake_username], USERPRINCIPALNAME(),
        collector_team_map[role_type], "MANAGER"
    )

// fct_delinquency filter (relationship: fct_delinquency -> dim_collector via
// assigned_collector_id, single-direction -- the RLS filter on dim_collector
// above propagates automatically to every fact table related to it, so NO
// separate filter expression is needed on fct_delinquency/fct_contact/
// fct_promise_to_pay themselves. This is the whole point of filtering the
// DIMENSION rather than repeating the filter logic on every fact table --
// same "define once" principle as the DAX base-measure pattern above.)

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Role: Collector
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// dim_collector filter:
[collector_id] =
    LOOKUPVALUE (
        collector_team_map[collector_id],
        collector_team_map[snowflake_username], USERPRINCIPALNAME(),
        collector_team_map[role_type], "COLLECTOR"
    )

// Same propagation-through-relationship note as Collections Manager above --
// a collector assigned this role sees ONLY their own accounts across every
// page, enforced once at the dim_collector table.

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Role: Compliance Auditor
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// No row filter (full visibility, matching Snowflake's ROLE_COMPLIANCE_AUDITOR
// grant on all tables) -- BUT every PII visual/table in this workbook uses a
// measure-level check to blank out phone/email/SSN fields for this role,
// since Power BI RLS operates on ROWS, not columns; column-level protection
// for PII (matching Snowflake's masking policies, Phase 12 Section 9) is
// implemented via Object-Level Security (OLS, via Tabular Editor -- not
// natively configurable in Power BI Desktop's RLS UI) restricting this
// role's visibility of phone_number/email/ssn_last4 columns entirely.

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Testing RLS: "View As Role" checklist (run before every publish)
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1. View As "Collector" + a specific test collector_id -> confirm EVERY
//    page's visuals show only that collector's assigned accounts, and the
//    Executive Summary page (Section 9) shows either blank or an
//    access-denied state (that page's visuals are bound to the KPI tables
//    a Collector role also can't see under the matching Snowflake grants --
//    consistency check between the two systems' access models).
// 2. View As "Executive" -> confirm zero rows render on any fact-bound
//    visual; only KPI-table-bound visuals (Executive Summary, Portfolio
//    Overview) show data.
// 3. View As "Collections Manager" -> confirm the visible account set
//    matches exactly the collectors on record for that manager's team in
//    collector_team_map, no more, no less.
